1
00:00:00,213 --> 00:00:03,593
A few objects become a landscape when they give you somewhere to
go.

2
00:00:03,669 --> 00:00:08,799
This woodland has a route, a clear destination, and scenery at
different distances.

3
00:00:09,003 --> 00:00:11,928
Let's build it from the small scene we made in the first lesson.

4
00:00:17,531 --> 00:00:23,599
We'll plan the route first, add a destination, and frame the space
with trees and smaller plants.

5
00:00:23,803 --> 00:00:28,214
Then we'll walk through it at player height. You can choose
different models and positions.

6
00:00:28,426 --> 00:00:32,365
What matters is whether your scene is easy to read and comfortable
to move through.

7
00:00:33,023 --> 00:00:36,894
Start with a copy of your first lesson save, so the original stays
available.

8
00:00:37,034 --> 00:00:40,490
Our copy still has the tree on the right and the rock on the left.

9
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:43,490
If you're continuing from another layout, that's fine.

10
00:00:43,544 --> 00:00:48,730
Keep a clear space between your two landmarks, and decide which
direction the route should continue.

11
00:00:49,527 --> 00:00:51,790
Look at the empty ground beyond the gap.

12
00:00:51,856 --> 00:00:58,094
Imagine a person walking from the starting area towards one
recognisable place. That line is our route.

13
00:00:58,170 --> 00:01:02,012
Leave enough space beside it for the character and the camera.
We're going to place

14
00:01:02,036 --> 00:01:06,326
scenery around this space, rather than filling it and trying to
cut a route through later.

15
00:01:07,018 --> 00:01:12,335
In the Place library, open Static Units, then Landform, and choose
Stone Road.

16
00:01:12,428 --> 00:01:18,453
This model is a group of separate flat stones. It gives us a
visual trail without covering the whole field.

17
00:01:18,699 --> 00:01:21,817
Check its name on the right so you know which object you're
editing.

18
00:01:21,972 --> 00:01:25,571
These are scenery objects; we aren't adding any gameplay logic to
them.

19
00:01:26,214 --> 00:01:32,038
Use Location to move the group towards the opening. Keep an eye on
the ground while you apply each change.

20
00:01:32,170 --> 00:01:35,336
A number in the panel tells you where the object's origin is;

21
00:01:35,370 --> 00:01:41,019
the visible model tells you whether it actually sits where you
intended. The example coordinates help us work,

22
00:01:41,044 --> 00:01:44,779
but you don't need to copy them. Check the placement from a lower
view as well.

23
00:01:46,342 --> 00:01:53,413
Now open Building and choose Rain Pavilion. Its roof and posts
make a small, recognisable destination.

24
00:01:53,638 --> 00:01:56,858
Put it beyond the tree and rock, where you can see it through the
opening.

25
00:01:57,030 --> 00:02:00,453
A focal point is simply the place you want someone to notice
first.

26
00:02:00,572 --> 00:02:04,151
It doesn't need to be the largest or most detailed object in the
scene.

27
00:02:04,899 --> 00:02:08,175
Check how much ground remains behind and around the pavilion.

28
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:13,295
If your destination is too close to the edge, bring it back before
adding more detail. Here,

29
00:02:13,320 --> 00:02:18,506
I move it closer to leave a more useful margin. We still need to
test the approach in play mode.

30
00:02:18,596 --> 00:02:22,407
An attractive editor view doesn't prove that the walking route
works.

31
00:02:25,913 --> 00:02:31,661
Add a few more Stone Road groups along the route. Move each new
group into place before adding another,

32
00:02:31,716 --> 00:02:37,382
so you can keep track of what is selected. Small changes in
spacing make the trail feel less mechanical.

33
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:42,249
Keep the intended direction readable. The grass between the groups
is part of this path;

34
00:02:42,304 --> 00:02:45,660
we're making a trail, rather than a perfectly tiled pavement.

35
00:03:01,842 --> 00:03:05,063
Use another Arbor to frame the opposite side of the approach.

36
00:03:05,193 --> 00:03:10,727
Select its placed instance, then adjust its location. Here, I keep
its original size.

37
00:03:10,910 --> 00:03:15,058
Leave the central opening visible, and compare its silhouette with
the first tree.

38
00:03:15,219 --> 00:03:17,711
Different models don't always need the same scale.

39
00:03:17,794 --> 00:03:23,535
If you resize a tree, keep all three Zoom values equal when you
want to preserve its proportions.

40
00:03:28,927 --> 00:03:34,130
Think in three distances. The foreground sits close to the viewer
and frames the entrance.

41
00:03:34,236 --> 00:03:36,848
The middle ground leads the eye along the trail.

42
00:03:37,010 --> 00:03:40,860
The background sits behind the destination and stops it looking
isolated.

43
00:03:41,063 --> 00:03:45,092
These are roles in the picture, rather than three exact distances
you must measure.

44
00:03:45,222 --> 00:03:49,270
We'll use a few trees at each distance, with open space between
the groups.

45
00:03:50,075 --> 00:03:52,964
Choose a different Arbor shape for some of the later trees.

46
00:03:53,104 --> 00:03:58,710
Change direction or size where it improves the outline, and leave
uneven gaps between the trunks.

47
00:03:58,835 --> 00:04:02,642
Repeating the same tree at identical intervals can look like a
fence.

48
00:04:02,803 --> 00:04:08,317
We want small groups with enough variation to feel natural, while
keeping the pavilion easy to find.

49
00:04:08,478 --> 00:04:11,392
More objects won't automatically make the composition better.

50
00:04:25,010 --> 00:04:28,326
Open Vegetation and place a Shrub near the edge of the trail.

51
00:04:28,423 --> 00:04:32,208
A low plant helps connect a large trunk to the ground around it.

52
00:04:32,306 --> 00:04:37,042
Check the selected name before changing the size, then move it
outside the walking space.

53
00:04:37,194 --> 00:04:42,387
Add only a few more. From a high editor view, even a small plant
can look harmless,

54
00:04:42,442 --> 00:04:44,826
so we'll check the edges of the route during our walk.

55
00:05:02,032 --> 00:05:05,889
Look towards the pavilion again. Can you still recognise its roof
and opening?

56
00:05:06,057 --> 00:05:10,931
If a tree or a branch hides the important part, move that piece of
scenery aside.

57
00:05:11,007 --> 00:05:15,778
Don't solve every gap by adding another object. Sometimes removing
an obstruction,

58
00:05:15,875 --> 00:05:20,094
or leaving a quiet patch of ground, makes the destination much
clearer.

59
00:05:20,909 --> 00:05:23,875
Lower the editing view to get a rough sense of the approach.

60
00:05:24,007 --> 00:05:30,118
This is useful for checking the composition, but it still isn't
the character's actual view or collision test.

61
00:05:30,322 --> 00:05:35,492
Notice how the nearby trees frame the route, and how the distant
trees sit behind the pavilion.

62
00:05:35,567 --> 00:05:38,137
We're keeping the existing light for this comparison,

63
00:05:38,213 --> 00:05:41,966
so a lighting change doesn't hide what the arrangement itself has
done.

64
00:05:42,756 --> 00:05:48,909
Quick Save, wait for the saved confirmation, and start a private
Test Play from the System Menu.

65
00:05:49,113 --> 00:05:54,433
Use the English test session, as in the first lesson. The saved
scene is our editing project;

66
00:05:54,615 --> 00:05:57,547
this test is where we check what the player can actually do.

67
00:05:57,645 --> 00:06:00,321
Wait for the character to appear before judging the route.

68
00:06:10,665 --> 00:06:15,623
Walk towards the opening, release the movement key to stop, and
look at the destination.

69
00:06:15,913 --> 00:06:21,206
Then continue along the stones. I'm checking that the route stays
on the ground, that the scenery leaves

70
00:06:21,231 --> 00:06:27,480
room to pass, and that the pavilion remains a useful landmark.
Check your own route at normal walking height.

71
00:06:27,568 --> 00:06:31,132
If something blocks you, return to the editor and move that
object.

72
00:06:39,770 --> 00:06:45,152
At the destination, stop and turn around. The scene should still
make sense from this direction.

73
00:06:45,352 --> 00:06:51,442
Look for an obvious way back, objects that seem to float, and
places where foliage cuts across the camera.

74
00:06:51,582 --> 00:06:54,962
A single attractive screenshot only covers one view.

75
00:06:55,122 --> 00:06:57,982
This short walk gives us a better check of the space we're
building.

76
00:06:58,813 --> 00:07:02,811
Compare the simple starting scene with the woodland from a similar
viewpoint.

77
00:07:02,951 --> 00:07:08,473
The useful difference is the relationship between the objects: the
stones suggest a route, the near

78
00:07:08,498 --> 00:07:14,576
trees frame it, and the background supports the destination. The
coordinates are still just one layout.

79
00:07:14,663 --> 00:07:18,854
You can make a wider clearing or a different focal point while
using the same ideas.

80
00:07:19,689 --> 00:07:25,447
Leave the private test, return to this lesson's editing save, and
Quick Save any final changes.

81
00:07:25,639 --> 00:07:28,166
Keep the finished copy available for the next episode.

82
00:07:28,284 --> 00:07:32,112
If you adjust the layout later, check the route again after the
change.

83
00:07:32,253 --> 00:07:37,058
Saving confirms the data was stored; walking through the space
checks a different part of the work.

84
00:07:46,121 --> 00:07:49,693
Before you move on, try this without looking at the recorded
numbers.

85
00:07:49,876 --> 00:07:54,451
Point out the route, the destination, and the three distances in
your own scene.

86
00:07:54,569 --> 00:07:57,685
Then walk from the start to the destination and back.

87
00:07:57,795 --> 00:08:02,716
If the direction is clear and you can move comfortably, you've
achieved the purpose of this lesson.

88
00:08:03,390 --> 00:08:05,532
Keep this woodland as your working scene.

89
00:08:05,566 --> 00:08:10,786
In the next lesson, we'll export a copy, import it separately, and
check what came across.

90
00:08:10,911 --> 00:08:16,338
That will give us a reliable way to share and recover the project
before we add the coin challenge.
